==== Neurodivergent-Specific Psychological Consequences
<subsubsec:neurodivergent-psychological-consequences>

The social and emotional dysfunction described above is amplified in people with pre-existing
neurodevelopmental conditions — autism, ADHD, and other forms of neurodivergence (ND). Autism and
ADHD alter the baseline energy cost of social interaction, identity formation, and diagnostic
recognition; ME/CFS adds a second, independent energy constraint on top of that baseline. The
result is a qualitatively different clinical picture in which neurodivergent coping strategies and
ME/CFS energy limits interact.

#clinical-finding(title: [Perceived Misdiagnosis of Fatigue-Related Conditions in Autistic Adults])[
  In a Dutch register survey of autistic adults (n=1,211), 24.6% reported at least one prior
  psychiatric diagnosis that they perceived as a misdiagnosis; personality, anxiety, and mood
  disorders were the most common, and chronic fatigue syndrome / burnout-related disorders were
  also explicitly listed, judged a misdiagnosis by 4% of the total sample
  @Kentrou2024autisticMisdiagnosis. The study captures diagnostic confusion / overshadowing around
  fatigue and energy symptoms in autistic adults, particularly women; it does not demonstrate that
  clinicians dismiss genuine ME/CFS as "just autism." *Certainty: 0.55.* (Large register survey;
  retrospective self-report; CFS/burnout grouped together; no ME/CFS-criteria validation.)

  *Falsifiable:* a prospective cohort of autistic adults presenting with new fatigue will show
  longer time-to-ME/CFS-diagnosis and lower rates of PEM assessment than matched non-autistic
  controls after adjusting for severity and healthcare access. See also the differential-diagnosis
  discussion in Section @sec:differential.
] <cf:neurodivergent-diagnostic-overshadowing>

#speculation(title: [Neurodivergent Masking as a Compounding Energy Cost])[
  *Certainty: 0.40 (Tier 2, convergent cross-domain evidence).*

  Autistic and ADHD camouflaging — the sustained suppression of stims, forced eye contact,
  rehearsed conversational scripts, and monitoring of socially expected responses — is itself a
  metabolically expensive self-regulation demand @Grant2026camouflagingCSS; @Hodge2026camouflagingMH.
  Research with healthy participants shows that sustained self-control depletes glucose and impairs
  subsequent self-regulation @Gailliot2007glucoseSelfControl; @Baumeister1998egoDepletion. In ME/CFS,
  where prefrontal metabolic reserves are already constrained, the *same* social encounter may
  require energy both for masking ME/CFS symptoms (hiding pain, flat affect, exhaustion) and for
  neurodivergent camouflaging (hiding autistic/ADHD traits). The two demands draw on the same
  prefrontal resource pool.

  *Consequence:* a neurodivergent person with ME/CFS may experience ordinary social interaction as
  disproportionately disabling because two independent high-cost self-regulation demands are
  superimposed, not because of greater psychological sensitivity. A clinician who recognises only
  one demand may misread the patient as anxious, avoidant, or non-compliant.

  *Falsifiable:* in a cohort stratified by ME/CFS status and neurodivergence status, ND+ME/CFS
  participants will report higher social-exertion cost and longer post-social recovery than
  neurotypical ME/CFS participants matched for overall disease severity, and this difference will
  correlate with self-reported masking burden. Falsified if no dose–response relationship exists
  between masking score and social-exertion cost across the four groups.
] <spec:neurodivergent-masking-double-burden>

#open-question(title: [Loss of Special Interests and Identity Disruption])[
  Autistic adults report that autistic burnout can involve loss of access to special interests,
  a change experienced as identity disruption and grief @Raymaker2020autisticBurnout;
  @Higgins2021burnoutDelphi. Whether ME/CFS energy loss produces a comparable ND-specific identity
  injury — the inability to sustain the focused activities that previously organized selfhood,
  regulation, and social connection — is unresolved. No study has compared autistic/ADHD people
  with and without ME/CFS on this dimension. *Falsifiable:* a mixed-methods cohort of autistic/ADHD
  adults with ME/CFS would show greater special-interest disruption and identity-grief scores than
  neurotypical ME/CFS controls, after adjusting for overall severity.
] <oq:neurodivergent-identity-loss>

#limitation(title: [Indirect Evidence: No Direct ME/CFS × Neurodivergence Cohort])[
  The neurodivergent-specific psychological-consequence claim rests on indirect evidence:
  autistic-burnout phenomenology, camouflaging cost in central-sensitivity syndromes, diagnostic
  overshadowing in autism, and ADHD–CFS transdiagnostic comparisons. No published study has
  prospectively assessed psychological outcomes in a cohort meeting both formal ME/CFS criteria
  and formal autism/ADHD criteria. Therefore:

  - Phenomenological overlap with autistic burnout must not be presented as evidence that the two
    conditions are the same.
  - Masking-cost extrapolation from autism/central-sensitivity research is mechanistically plausible
    but unmeasured in ME/CFS.
  - Identity-loss and diagnostic-overshadowing claims are hypothesis-generating, not established.
] <lim:neurodivergent-evidence-directness>

*For clinicians:* When an autistic or ADHD patient presents with fatigue, social withdrawal, or
executive decline, evaluate for ME/CFS independently of the neurodevelopmental diagnosis. PEM
history, two-day CPET where feasible, and orthostatic assessment should not be skipped on the
assumption that the symptoms are "just" autism or ADHD. Conversely, when a patient with ME/CFS
has autistic traits or ADHD, social demands and masking expectations should be lowered more
aggressively than for neurotypical patients, because two energy costs are layered.

*For patients:* If you are autistic or ADHD and your fatigue feels different from your baseline —
especially if exertion (mental, physical, or social) triggers delayed, multi-day crashes — that may
reflect ME/CFS layered on top of neurodivergence rather than a worsening of the neurodivergence
itself. The distinction matters for pacing, for accessing ME/CFS-specific care, and for explaining
your experience to others.
